FILE = "germany-adidas-farewell-5-0.html"
TITLE = "Germany say goodbye to Adidas with a 5-0 win over the Netherlands"
TAG = "Flavor Sports"
H1 = "Germany sign off from Adidas with a 5-0 win"
DEK = "More than 70 years in the three stripes ended on Monday night in Berlin, with Germany's biggest win over the Dutch since 1959 and a Nations League quarter-final."
META = "Flavor Sports · 17 Nov 2026 · 3 min read"
LEAD = ("img/sp-germany-farewell-team.jpg", "Germany line up in the black farewell kit at the Olympiastadion", "DFB / Philipp Reinhard")

BODY = [
("p", "Germany's men played their last game in Adidas on Monday, 16 November 2026, and they made it a night to remember. Jürgen Klopp's side beat the Netherlands 5-0 at the Olympiastadion in Berlin to finish top of their Nations League group and book a place in the quarter-finals."),
("p", "From 1 January 2027, Nike takes over as the national team's kit supplier. So this was the final outing for the black-and-gold farewell shirt, and the last time the German men walked out in the three stripes."),
("h2", "The game", "A 5-0 ", "send-off"),
("p", "Derry Scherhant opened the scoring just before half-time. The winger was born in Berlin and came through at Hertha, the club that calls the Olympiastadion home, and it was his first goal for Germany."),
("p", "The second half was one-way traffic. Florian Wirtz scored the second, Nico Schlotterbeck added a third and Wirtz got his second of the night for 4-0. Brajan Gruda, who had already set up two, finished it off with the fifth."),
("img", "img/sp-germany-scherhant.jpg", "Derry Scherhant celebrates his first goal for Germany", "DFB / Philipp Reinhard"),
("facts", [("Score", "Germany 5-0 Netherlands"), ("Venue", "Olympiastadion, Berlin"), ("Germany scorers", "Wirtz 2, Scherhant, Schlotterbeck, Gruda"), ("Group A2", "Germany 1st, Netherlands 2nd, Greece 3rd")]),
("h2", "Why it matters", "A first for Xavi, ", "a statement for Klopp"),
("p", "It was Germany's biggest win over their neighbours since a 7-0 in 1959. It was also the first defeat for the Netherlands since Xavi Hernández took over as their coach in August."),
("p", "Germany needed it. They started the night third in Group A2 and had to win to stay in the race. Greece beat Serbia 1-0 in the other game, which left Germany, the Netherlands and Greece all on 11 points, and the win in Berlin put Germany top. The Netherlands still go through in second, while Greece miss out."),
("p", "The defence deserves a mention too. Germany conceded just three goals in their six group games."),
("quote", "Everyone who was here today is going home with a smile, and won't be questioning the German national team for the next three or four months.", "Jürgen Klopp, after the match"),
("img", "img/sp-germany-celebration.jpg", "Germany's players celebrate in Berlin", "DFB / Philipp Reinhard"),
("h2", "The kit story", "Since 1954, ", "and now the end"),
("p", "Adidas and German football go back to the 1954 World Cup in Bern, when Adi Dassler supplied the team's boots, the ones with screw-in studs, for the final Germany won against Hungary. Adidas went on to supply the full kit from 1980."),
("p", "In those colours, Germany's men won four World Cups and three European Championships, and the women won two World Cups. That history is why the switch to Nike, announced by the German football federation (DFB) in March 2024, landed as much more than a business story in Germany. The DFB said the decision came down to money."),
("p", "Even Klopp was part of the send-off. His Adidas cap reading \"One of 83 million\", a nod to Germany's population, has been a fixture on the touchline since he took the job, and he wore it again on Monday. He'll need a new one next year."),
("p", "We looked at the farewell shirt itself when it came out: <a href=\"germany-final-adidas-kit.html\">End of an era: Germany's last Adidas shirt</a>. And for more on why football shirts have become fashion, read <a href=\"kit-culture.html\">Kit Culture</a>."),
]

